Group 2 -  Initial Assessment Team (IAT)
 Member 1 (**Emergency Medicine Physician (Lead)**): Responsible for the initial evaluation of the patient's acute condition, managing the urgent aspects of the case such as stabilization, resuscitation, and identification of the most likely cause of symptoms.
 Member 2 (**Pediatric Infectious Disease Specialist**): Focuses on assessing the patient for potential infectious causes, such as bacterial or viral infections that could explain the fever, rash, and altered mental status.
 Member 3 (**Obstetrician/Gynecologist**): Evaluates the patient’s gynecological history and current situation, particularly addressing the heavy menstrual bleeding and potential complications related to retained tampons, such as toxic shock syndrome.

Group 3 -  Diagnostic Evidence Team (DET)
 Member 1 (**Clinical Microbiologist (Lead)**): Analyzes laboratory results (blood cultures, swabs) to identify any underlying infections that might explain the presentation and assists in guiding appropriate antimicrobial therapy.
 Member 2 (**Radiologist**): Interprets imaging studies if required to look for any source of infection, abscesses, or other complications that could arise from the initial diagnosis.
 Member 3 (**Hematologist**): Reviews blood tests related to the patient’s coagulopathy and bleeding, providing insights on any disorders that might complicate management and the potential hematological implications of toxic shock syndrome (TSS).

Group 4 -  Final Review and Decision Team (FRDT)
 Member 1 (**Senior Consultant Physician (Lead)**): Coordinates decision
 Member 2 (**Clinical Pharmacologist**): Reviews and recommends the best pharmacological treatments based on the integrated findings, ensuring safe and effective medication management for infection and hemorrhage.
 Member 3 (**Nursing Coordinator**): Ensures seamless communication between the various specialties, oversees the implementation of the management plan, and acts as the liaison for patient care, ensuring all aspects of treatment are executed effectively.


